{"evidence_status":"partially_supported","requires_refinement":true,"answer":"The evidence partially supports the hypothesis: 72 of 256 universes show a statistically significant positive association, while none show a statistically significant negative association. However, 184 universes are indeterminate, and inference changes substantially across some outcome, exposure-model, adjustment, and skin-tone-encoding choices, so the broad claim is not uniformly established.","rationale":"Positive significant results outnumber contrary significant results (72 versus 0), and all 256 estimates have confidence intervals, but the large indeterminate share and high inference-flip rates prevent robust support. The evidence also mixes distinct outcome definitions and treatment contrasts rather than testing one identical dark-versus-light claim throughout.","key_evidence":["72 of 256 universes have significant positive effects; 0 have significant negative effects.","184 of 256 universes are classified as indeterminate, although no universes are unavailable and all 256 have confidence intervals.","Inference flip rates are 51.56% for straight-red-only versus all-sendings-off outcomes and 31.08% for fixed-covariate adjustment choices.","Median standardized effects differ across adjustment choices, from 0.000059 for player-characteristics/referee-bias adjustment to 1.201 unadjusted; exposure-model medians range from 0.508 to 1.579.],"
