,"refinement_reason":"The multiverse combines different red-card outcomes, event/exposure estimands, and skin-tone representations. These choices define substantively different claims, especially because some specifications use continuous or multi-level skin-tone predictors rather than a direct dark-versus-light contrast.","refinement_dimensions":["Outcome definition: all sendings-off versus straight red cards only","Event/exposure estimand: count rate, any-event probability, versus exposure-adjusted event rate","Skin-tone measurement and encoding: binary dark-versus-light versus continuous or five-level representations"],"suggested_hypotheses":["Referees are more likely to award any sending-off, including second-yellow sendings-off, to dark-skinned than to light-skinned players under a prespecified binary skin-tone contrast.","Referees are more likely to award straight red cards to dark-skinned than to light-skinned players under a prespecified binary skin-tone contrast.","The rate of sending-offs per game is higher for dark-skinned than for light-skinned players under a prespecified exposure-adjusted count model."],"caveats":["The supplied summary does not report the number or distribution of non-significant results separately from the 184 indeterminate results.","Standardized effects from different outcome and exposure models are not necessarily directly comparable.","High inference-flip rates indicate sensitivity to specification choices, even though the reported sign-reversal rates are zero.","The evidence is associational; the supplied results do not establish that skin tone causes referee decisions."]}
